    <section id="principles" class="principles section-shell section-space" aria-labelledby="principles-title">
        <div class="section-heading">
            <p class="eyebrow">STRONG DEFAULTS, OPEN EDGES</p>
            <h2 id="principles-title">Clear boundaries.<br />Less glue.</h2>
            <p>NOF coordinates the hard parts of distributed applications without turning your domain into framework code.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="principle-grid">
            <article class="principle-card principle-card-blue">
                <span class="card-index">01</span>
                <div class="card-symbol" aria-hidden="true">{ }</div>
                <h3>Contract first</h3>
                <p>Define typed RPC contracts once. NOF generates clients, servers, HTTP endpoints, and dispatch wiring at compile time.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Typed request and response models</li>
                    <li>Streaming RPC with SSE</li>
                    <li>AOT-aware serialization paths</li>
                </ul>
            </article>

            <article class="principle-card principle-card-dark">
                <span class="card-index">02</span>
                <div class="card-symbol" aria-hidden="true">→□</div>
                <h3>Reliable by design</h3>
                <p>Inbox/outbox, ordered delivery, commands, and notifications are part of one model—not a collection of afterthoughts.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Transactional message dispatch</li>
                    <li>RabbitMQ and in-memory transports</li>
                    <li>Idempotent inbound processing</li>
                </ul>
            </article>

            <article class="principle-card principle-card-paper">
                <span class="card-index">03</span>
                <div class="card-symbol" aria-hidden="true">◎</div>
                <h3>Operationally legible</h3>
                <p>Multi-tenancy, authentication, startup sequencing, and telemetry share the same runtime vocabulary.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>OpenTelemetry instrumentation</li>
                    <li>Dependency-aware initialization</li>
                    <li>Tenant-aware persistence</li>
                </ul>
            </article>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section id="architecture" class="architecture section-space" aria-labelledby="architecture-title">
        <div class="section-shell architecture-inner">
            <div class="architecture-copy">
                <p class="eyebrow eyebrow-light">A SYSTEM YOU CAN REASON ABOUT</p>
                <h2 id="architecture-title">One mental model,<br />from domain to runtime.</h2>
                <p>
                    Each layer has a job. Dependencies point inward. Infrastructure stays replaceable,
                    while source generators keep the boundaries fast and explicit.
                </p>
                <a class="text-link" href="design/">Explore the design notes <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
            </div>

            <div class="layer-stack" aria-label="NOF framework layers">
                <article class="layer layer-hosting">
                    <span>04</span><div><strong>Hosting</strong><small>ASP.NET Core · Blazor · Console · MAUI</small></div><i>edge</i>
                </article>
                <article class="layer layer-infrastructure">
                    <span>03</span><div><strong>Infrastructure</strong><small>Persistence · Messaging · Auth · Telemetry</small></div><i>adapters</i>
                </article>
                <article class="layer layer-application">
                    <span>02</span><div><strong>Application</strong><small>RPC · Handlers · State machines · Pipelines</small></div><i>use cases</i>
                </article>
                <article class="layer layer-domain">
                    <span>01</span><div><strong>Domain</strong><small>Value objects · Failures · Business rules</small></div><i>core</i>
                </article>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="capabilities section-shell section-space" aria-labelledby="capabilities-title">
        <div class="section-heading section-heading-row">
            <div>
                <p class="eyebrow">BATTERIES, COORDINATED</p>
                <h2 id="capabilities-title">Built for systems<br />that have to last.</h2>
            </div>
            <p>Adopt the complete runtime or choose focused packages. The conventions stay consistent across the stack.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="capability-list">
            <article><span class="capability-number">01</span><h3>CQRS & messaging</h3><p>Typed commands, notifications, RPC, streaming, and handler pipelines.</p><span class="capability-mark">↗</span></article>
            <article><span class="capability-number">02</span><h3>Source generation</h3><p>Move registration and transport plumbing from runtime reflection to compile time.</p><span class="capability-mark">⌁</span></article>
            <article><span class="capability-number">03</span><h3>Transactional data</h3><p>EF Core and NHibernate integration with inbox/outbox and unit-of-work semantics.</p><span class="capability-mark">◇</span></article>
            <article><span class="capability-number">04</span><h3>Identity & tenancy</h3><p>Authorization, OIDC endpoints, propagated context, and tenant-aware persistence.</p><span class="capability-mark">◎</span></article>
            <article><span class="capability-number">05</span><h3>State machines</h3><p>Declarative transitions with persistent state and event-driven workflows.</p><span class="capability-mark">⇄</span></article>
            <article><span class="capability-number">06</span><h3>Observability</h3><p>Tracing, metrics, and structured logs that follow work across service boundaries.</p><span class="capability-mark">∿</span></article>
        </div>
    </section>

